        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="15.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#diy-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. DIY Cost per Square Foot</a>
          <a href="#tools" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Tools and Extras</a>
          <a href="#professional" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Contractor Pricing</a>
          <a href="#drivers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. What Moves the Price</a>
          <a href="#ten-year" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Ten-Year Number</a>
          <a href="#examples" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Three Worked Patios</a>
          <a href="#worth-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Where the Money Goes Best</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every cost guide answers the wrong question. What it costs to seal a patio once is rarely the number that matters, because a patio sealed once and then abandoned looks worse than one never sealed at all.
        </p>

        <div id="diy-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. DIY Cost per Square Foot
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer is the whole material cost, and it varies more by chemistry than by brand.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price per Gallon</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coverage per Coat</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Two-Coat Cost per sq ft</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$30-50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150-200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.30-0.65</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$45-75</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150-200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.45-1.00</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-300 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.27-0.70 (one coat)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint stabilising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50-85</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.65-1.70</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Anti-slip additive</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$15-25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Treats 5 gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.02-0.04</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For a typical patio with a mid-range water-based acrylic at two coats, budget around $0.35 to $0.55 per square foot in sealer. A 500 square foot patio therefore costs roughly $175 to $275 in product. That is the honest DIY number for most people, and it is considerably lower than most published estimates because those usually assume a contractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage is the variable that moves this most, and it depends on paver porosity rather than on the product. Old, worn or rough-textured pavers can drink at 100 square feet per gallon on the first coat where smooth new ones give 200. The first coat always goes further or shorter than you expect and the second is more predictable, so buy for the pessimistic end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joint stabilising sealers cost more per square foot for two reasons: the product is more expensive, and coverage is lower because a meaningful proportion soaks into the joints rather than sitting on the pavers. If your reason for sealing is joint sand rather than appearance, budget roughly double.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not forget the cleaning products. A patio cleaner, a moss treatment and possibly an efflorescence or rust remover add $30 to $80 depending on condition, and skipping the cleaning to save that is how you end up sealing dirt permanently under a film.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Tools and Extras
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mostly one-time purchases, and one of them is the most common mid-job frustration in the whole task.
          </p>

          <p>
            A solvent-resistant pump sprayer is $40 to $80 and is genuinely necessary if you are using a solvent-based sealer. An ordinary garden sprayer's seals dissolve, usually about a third of the way across the patio, which leaves you with a set edge you cannot blend into. This is the single most common avoidable expense, because people buy the cheap sprayer and then buy the right one anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            A short-nap roller, a frame and an extension pole come to $25 to $45. Use 1/4 or 3/8 inch nap and one rated for solvents if applicable, because a long-nap roller holds too much product and applies it too thick, which is the main cause of a cloudy finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pressure washer is the big one, and it is usually already owned or rented. A rental is $60 to $100 a day; an electric machine in the 1,500 to 2,000 PSI range is $150 to $300. For paver work you want moderate pressure and a wide tip, so a powerful petrol machine is not an advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then the sundries: polymeric sand if the joints need topping up at $25 to $40 a bag covering roughly 25 to 100 square feet of joints depending on width, plus gloves, eye protection and sheeting for adjacent surfaces at $30 to $50. Sand is the item most often forgotten at the planning stage and most often needed once the patio is washed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">One-Time Tool Outlay</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$40-80</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Solvent-resistant sprayer</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$25-45</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Roller, frame, pole</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$60-100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pressure washer, per day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$30-50</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">PPE and sheeting</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="professional" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Contractors Charge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Professional pricing is usually quoted per square foot and bundles cleaning, sanding and sealing, which is why comparing it directly to a bottle of sealer is misleading.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Service</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical per sq ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">On 500 sq ft</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.30-0.70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$150-350</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seal only, surface already clean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.70-1.40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$350-700</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean, sand and seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.00-2.50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$500-1,250</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean, strip old sealer, re-seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2.00-4.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000-2,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Re-sand with polymeric, add $0.30-0.70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">included above</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">-</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most quotes land between $1.00 and $2.50 per square foot for the full service. Below $0.80 is worth scrutinising, because the corners cut are usually the ones that decide the outcome: insufficient drying time between cleaning and sealing, one heavy coat instead of two thin ones, or skipping the joint sand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask four specific questions on any quote. How long between cleaning and sealing, since anything under 24 hours is a risk. How many coats, because one is not enough. Whether polymeric sand is included and whether it will be cured before sealing. And which product, so you can look up whether it is a film or penetrating type and whether it matches what you want.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gap between $0.45 of DIY material and $1.50 of contractor pricing is labour, equipment and the risk they carry if it goes wrong. That is not unreasonable, and paver sealing is a job where a professional's main advantage is not skill so much as the discipline to wait for the surface to be properly dry when the customer wants it finished today.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing worth paying for if you do hire: ask whether they will apply an anti-slip additive, and insist on it around pools and steps. Many will not offer it unprompted, and it is far easier to add at application than afterwards, as our guide on whether sealing makes pavers slippery explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Moves the Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            In rough order of impact on both DIY and contracted work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paver Porosity and Condition</h3>

          <p>
            The single biggest material variable. Old, rough, weathered pavers can use half again as much sealer as smooth new ones, and they usually need more cleaning first. A neglected patio costs more to seal than a maintained one on both counts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whether There Is Existing Sealer</h3>

          <p>
            The biggest single jump in a contractor quote. A patio with a sound compatible film can be recoated. One with a degraded, flaking or unknown coating has to be stripped first, which can roughly double the price and is genuinely unpleasant to do yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealer Chemistry</h3>

          <p>
            Solvent-based costs more per gallon than water-based, joint stabilising costs more again and covers less. Choosing a wet look solvent product over a basic water-based acrylic can add 50 percent to the material bill on the same patio.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Condition</h3>

          <p>
            Joints that need re-sanding add both material and a full day of waiting for the polymeric sand to cure before sealing. On a patio that has not been maintained, this is frequently a larger cost than the sealer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Access and Site Protection</h3>

          <p>
            A patio surrounded by planting, adjacent to render or siding, or with a pool alongside needs significant masking, and on a contracted job that is real labour. It affects DIY mostly in time rather than money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ten-year" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Ten-Year Number, Which Is the One That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the calculation almost nobody does before starting, and it is the one that should drive the decision.
          </p>

          <p>
            Film sealers last roughly two to three years in high-exposure areas and up to five in sheltered ones. Penetrating sealers typically last three to five. So over ten years you are looking at somewhere between two and five applications, plus quite likely one strip when the film has built up or degraded past recoating. Our guide on how often to seal pavers covers the intervals in detail.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approach</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Applications in 10 yrs</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIY 10-yr Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pro 10-yr Cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic, 3-yr cycle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-1,100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,800-4,400</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent wet look, 2-3 yr cycle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$900-1,800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,400-5,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating, 4-5 yr cycle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$350-700</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,200-2,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plus one strip and reseal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">add 1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">add $200-400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">add $1,000-2,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Never seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those figures are for a 500 square foot patio. The pattern is what matters: a decade of DIY wet look sealing is roughly $900 to $1,800, and the same done professionally is $2,400 to $5,500. Neither is unreasonable for something you look at daily, but both are far larger than the single-application figure people have in mind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note how well penetrating sealers do on this measure. They cost similar money per application, last longer, do not build up into a film that eventually needs stripping, and do not create the slip problem. If your reason for sealing is stain protection rather than the wet look, they are substantially cheaper over a decade.
          </p>

          <p>
            And note the last row. Not sealing is free, and it is a legitimate option for a patio you are happy with. Our guide on whether pavers need sealing makes the case properly, and the honest summary is that the structural argument for sealing does not exist; the aesthetic and stain-resistance arguments do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Three Worked Patios
          </h2>

          <p>
            DIY materials against a typical contractor quote for the same work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patio 1: 300 sq ft, Good Condition, Water-Based Acrylic</h3>

          <p>
            Clean with a $20 cleaner, two coats of a $40-a-gallon water-based acrylic at 175 square feet per coat needs about 3.5 gallons, so $140. Sundries $40. Total around $200, or $0.67 per square foot including cleaning. A contractor quoting clean-and-seal at $1.20 would be about $360. DIY is a comfortable Saturday and Sunday.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patio 2: 600 sq ft, Weathered, Solvent Wet Look, Joints Need Sand</h3>

          <p>
            Cleaner and moss treatment $60. Three bags of polymeric sand $105. Two coats of a $60-a-gallon solvent acrylic at 150 square feet per coat needs 8 gallons, so $480. Solvent-resistant sprayer $60, anti-slip additive $20, sundries $50. Total around $775, or $1.29 per square foot. A contractor at $1.80 including sand would be about $1,080. The gap narrows because the material is the expensive part here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patio 3: 800 sq ft, Existing Failing Sealer</h3>

          <p>
            This is the expensive case. Stripper for 800 square feet at roughly $0.50 per square foot is $400, plus cleaning $60, plus 11 gallons of new sealer at $55 is $605, plus sundries and additive $100. Total around $1,165 in materials alone, plus two full weekends including the strip. A contractor quoting strip-and-reseal at $3.00 would be about $2,400. This is the patio where you seriously consider not resealing at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pattern across the three is that DIY saves most on simple jobs and least on material-heavy ones, and that an existing failed sealer roughly doubles everything. That last point is the practical argument for either maintaining a seal on schedule or never starting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where the Money Goes Best
          </h2>

          <p>
            Given that this is recurring spending, some choices return far more than others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend on the right chemistry for your goal. If you want stain protection and do not specifically want the wet look, a penetrating sealer costs less over a decade, lasts longer, never needs stripping and does not make the surface slippery. A great deal of money is spent on film sealers by people whose actual requirement was stain resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend on the solvent-resistant sprayer if you are using solvent product. Forty to eighty dollars against the near-certainty of a failed sprayer mid-patio and a visible set edge is not a close call.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend the fifteen to twenty-five dollars on anti-slip additive if there is any step, slope or pool involved. It is the cheapest line on the whole budget and the only one with a safety consequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Save on the pressure washer by renting, and by not buying a petrol machine. Paver work wants moderate pressure and a wide tip; more power is a liability that blasts out joint sand and etches paver faces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Save by doing the cleaning yourself and hiring out the sealing, if the budget is tight and you want a professional finish. Cleaning is the labour-heavy, low-skill part and it is where a contractor's hourly rate buys you the least.
          </p>

          <p>
            And save the most by deciding deliberately at the outset. A patio sealed on a whim, neglected for six years and then stripped costs more than a decade of maintained sealing, and considerably more than never having started. The expensive mistakes in this area are all failures of commitment rather than failures of product choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Budgeting for One Application Instead of a Decade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Film sealers last two to three years in exposed areas and up to five when sheltered, so ten years means three to five applications plus probably one strip. A 500 square foot patio runs $900 to $1,800 DIY over that period and $2,400 to $5,500 professionally, which is the number that should drive the decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Ordinary Pump Sprayer for Solvent Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent dissolves the seals in a standard garden sprayer, typically about a third of the way across the patio, leaving a set edge that cannot be blended. People almost always buy the cheap one and then buy the right one anyway, so the forty to eighty dollars is not really optional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Cleaning Products to Save Money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sealer locks whatever is on the surface under a film permanently. Thirty to eighty dollars of cleaner, moss treatment and stain remover is trivial against the cost of stripping a patio because you sealed dirt into it. This is the worst possible place to economise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Film Sealer When You Wanted Stain Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating sealers cost similar money per application, last longer, never build up into a film needing stripping, and do not make the surface slippery. A lot of money goes on wet look products bought by people whose actual requirement was resisting oil and wine, which a penetrating sealer handles.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting a Sealed Patio Go Past Its Interval</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A film left to degrade wears unevenly into patchy glossy and dull areas, and eventually cannot be recoated, only stripped. Stripping roughly doubles the cost of the next cycle. Maintaining on schedule, or never starting, are both cheaper than sealing once and neglecting it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to seal pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DIY materials run roughly $0.35 to $0.55 per square foot for a two-coat water-based acrylic, so about $175 to $275 on a 500 square foot patio. Contractors typically charge $1.00 to $2.50 per square foot for clean, sand and seal, which is $500 to $1,250 on the same patio.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to seal pavers yourself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Substantially, on simple jobs. A 300 square foot patio in good condition is around $200 in materials against roughly $360 from a contractor. The gap narrows on material-heavy jobs using expensive solvent products, and closes further if the patio needs an existing failed sealer stripped first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much sealer do I need for 500 square feet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For two coats at 150 to 200 square feet per gallon per coat, between 5 and 6.7 gallons. Buy for the pessimistic end, because old, rough or weathered pavers drink far more on the first coat than smooth new ones. Penetrating sealers cover more, often 200 to 300 square feet, and usually need one coat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are paver sealing quotes so different from each other?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because they include different work. Ask specifically how long between cleaning and sealing, how many coats, whether polymeric sand is included and cured before sealing, and which product. A quote below about $0.80 per square foot is usually cutting one of those, and they are the ones that decide the outcome.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does it cost to strip old paver sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly $0.50 per square foot in stripper alone for DIY, so about $400 on an 800 square foot patio, plus the new sealer on top. Contractors typically charge $2.00 to $4.00 per square foot for strip-and-reseal. It roughly doubles the cost of a cycle, which is the argument for maintaining on schedule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are penetrating sealers cheaper over time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. They cost similar money per application but last three to five years rather than two to three, cover more per gallon, and never build into a film that eventually needs stripping. Over a decade on a 500 square foot patio the DIY difference is roughly $350 to $700 against $900 to $1,800.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing pavers add value to a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It improves appearance, which affects presentation rather than appraised value in any measurable way. Treat it as maintenance and enjoyment spending rather than an investment. The stain resistance on a driveway or grill area has genuine practical value; the wet look is aesthetic, which is a fine reason on its own.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

